Reads the base gateway JSON (listener, issuer, session key; services optional),
adds one form-source service for every MCPServer with auth.mode header and an
auth.credentialGateway block, and applies the result like "apply". The headers
a server declares in auth.headers become the sign-in inputs, so pushing the
server's .mcp/servers.yaml and running sync configures the gateway. Each
derived service reads its resource-client key from client.pem, supplied with
--secret-file client.pem=<file>. Applying restarts mcp-auth, which clears
header values people submitted at sign-in; they sign in again.

Usage:
  mcp-runtime cluster mcp-auth-gateway sync [flags]

Flags:
      --context string             Explicit Kubernetes context
      --dry-run                    Validate inputs and show the configuration plan without writing
      --expect-cluster-id string   Expected kube-system namespace UID
      --file string                Gateway JSON file; references must use /etc/mcp-auth-gateway/<key>
  -h, --help                       help for sync
      --kubeconfig string          Explicit kubeconfig path
      --mappings-file string       Private identity-to-vault-reference mapping JSON file (only for vault services)
      --mcp-host string            Public MCP host for servers that set no ingressHost
      --secret-file stringArray    Mount a private KEY=FILE input (repeat for provider token, session key and resource-client key)
      --tls-secret string          Existing TLS Secret covering the auth hostname; no certificate is issued
      --yes                        Apply the reviewed configuration without another prompt

Global Flags:
      --debug   Enable debug mode with structured error logging
